  <question type="multianswer">
    <name><text>B6 - Set operations: De Morgan &amp; cardinality (Cloze)</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Operations on Sets]</strong> Work in a universe \(U\) with \(|U| = 40\).</p>
<p>(a) By De Morgan's law, the complement of \(A \cap B\) equals {1:MULTICHOICE:=Aᶜ ∪ Bᶜ~Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ~A ∪ B~Aᶜ ∪ B}&nbsp; (here Xᶜ denotes the complement of X).</p>
<p>(b) If \(|A| = 13\), then \( |\overline{A}| = \) {2:NUMERICAL:=27:0}.</p>
<p>(c) The number of subsets of a set with 5 elements is {1:NUMERICAL:=32:0}.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) De Morgan: \( \overline{A \cap B} = \overline{A} \cup \overline{B} \). (b) \( |\overline{A}| = 40 - 13 = 27 \). (c) \( 2^5 = 32 \).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
  </question>

  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>B7 - Relations: properties of a divisibility relation</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Relations]</strong> Define a relation \(R\) on \( \mathbb{Z}^{+} \) (positive integers) by \( a\,R\,b \iff a \mid b \) (\(a\) divides \(b\)). Select <em>all</em> properties that \(R\) satisfies.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>\(a\mid a\) (reflexive); if \(a\mid b\) and \(b\mid a\) then \(a=b\) on \(\mathbb{Z}^+\) (antisymmetric); if \(a\mid b\) and \(b\mid c\) then \(a\mid c\) (transitive). So \(R\) is a partial order — it is <em>not</em> symmetric (e.g. \(2\mid 4\) but \(4\nmid 2\)).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>false</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <correctfeedback format="html"><text>Correct.</text></correctfeedback>
    <partiallycorrectfeedback format="html"><text>Partially correct.</text></partiallycorrectfeedback>
    <incorrectfeedback format="html"><text>Incorrect.</text></incorrectfeedback>
    <answer fraction="33.33333" format="html"><text>Reflexive</text><feedback format="html"><text>Yes.</text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="-100" format="html"><text>Symmetric</text><feedback format="html"><text>No — \(2\mid 4\) but \(4 \nmid 2\).</text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="33.33333" format="html"><text>Antisymmetric</text><feedback format="html"><text>Yes.</text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="33.33333" format="html"><text>Transitive</text><feedback format="html"><text>Yes.</text></feedback></answer>
  </question>

  <question type="shortanswer">
    <name><text>B8 - Functions: classify f(x)=x^2 on R</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Functions]</strong> Consider \( f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R},\ f(x)=x^2 \). In one word, is it injective, surjective, or neither? (Type the single best answer.)</p>]]></text>
