  <!-- ============ PART III — PROOFS (Q11-Q12) ============ -->

  <question type="essay">
    <name><text>B11 - Proof by contradiction: sqrt(3) is irrational</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Proof — Contradiction]</strong> Prove that \( \sqrt{3} \) is irrational using a proof by contradiction. State your assumption clearly, derive a contradiction, and conclude.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Model proof.</strong> Assume \( \sqrt{3} = \dfrac{a}{b} \) with \( \gcd(a,b)=1 \), \(b\neq 0\). Then \( a^2 = 3b^2 \), so \(3 \mid a^2\), and since 3 is prime, \(3 \mid a\): write \(a = 3k\). Then \(9k^2 = 3b^2 \Rightarrow b^2 = 3k^2\), so \(3 \mid b\). Thus \(3\) divides both \(a\) and \(b\), contradicting \( \gcd(a,b)=1 \). Hence \( \sqrt{3} \) is irrational. \( \blacksquare \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
