  <question type="essay">
    <name><text>B12 - Proof by contraposition: n^2 odd implies n odd</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Proof — Contraposition]</strong> Let \( n \in \mathbb{Z} \). Prove that <em>if \( n^2 \) is odd then \( n \) is odd</em>, using a proof by contraposition. State the contrapositive you will prove, then prove it.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Model proof.</strong> The contrapositive is: "if \(n\) is even then \(n^2\) is even." Assume \(n\) is even, so \(n = 2k\). Then \( n^2 = (2k)^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2) \), which is even. Hence the contrapositive holds, so the original statement "\(n^2\) odd \(\Rightarrow n\) odd" is true. \( \blacksquare \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
